dunric@yahoo.com wrote:
> Then it dawned on me. Gustav had accidentally stumbled upon
> a compiled Fortran program compressed by the Impossibly Efficient
> "Zeus Compression Algorithm" (ZCA). That is, a compression algo-
> rithm so efficient that pages upon pages of text can be compressed
> into a single byte. The ZCA, in theory, can compress the entire
> contents of the Holy Bible into a single byte.

The question of whether such compression is actually possible --
contrary to what you may hear in the literature -- is NOT a
mathematical question, but actually an empirical issue. The question at
hand is whether the Universe, itself, is of such a nature and structure
that it (and the various parts of it, including texts written within
it) can be efficiently encoded -- even delineated in its entirety -- by
nothing more than a few bytes.

For all we know, it may very well have a substantial hidden order in
it, of this nature. But, as of yet, nobody's ever found it or anything
like it. Nonetheless, the very possibility automatically excludes any
"mathematical" argument that extreme compression is impossible. At
best, you will have a mathematical argument based on some (rather
general) empirical assumptions about the predictability of the universe
and the parts comprising it.

The question is most definitely not a triviality, as is demonstrated
(for instance) by the extreme predictability of human beings and the
various collectives formed therefrom (such as societies, cultures,
etc.).



