I disagree.  We have yet to see the compression algorithm.
There must be some means of deciding whether the input
is the Bible or some other text.  I can't offhand see a way
short of having a reference copy of the Bible built into the
compression program (or available to it).

As for decompression, I again see no possibility other than
to have a reference copy of the Bible available to the
decompression program.  It is left as an exercise for the
reader to determine how to distribute these reference
copies to insure that the compression and decompression
programs use the same one.

So, I ask again: in what way does this constitute a compression
if copies of the text are required independently of the "compressed"
data.  It seems to me to require *more* space: the space required
to store the reference copy, the space taken up by the compression
and decompression programs, and the bit taken up by the so-called
"compressed" text.

-- 
J. Giles

