:cards are M7621 and MS650-Bx are M7622, so by definition any M7622 card
:would work with the III+. Can you confirm that?

  The modules that meet the revision minimums are expected to work.

  As for the root of the question, ayup, looks like it.  The M numbers
  did tend to be unique, save for a few part-populated modules I remember
  seeing.  MS650-A* does look to be M7621, and MS650-B* looks to be M7622.
  The M7621-%% and M7622-%% module variants are semiconductor supplier
  variants, as I recall.

  Do remember Google, too, as you can obviously find a wealth of old
  MicroVAX hardware information using that tool.

  A quick check of Google found the following URLs:

    http://cmcnabb.cc.vt.edu/dec94mds/ka650rvc.txt
    http://cmcnabb.cc.vt.edu/dec94mds/ka655x01.txt

  ...among various other hits for a search for the keywords M7621
  and M7622.  Better -- and faster -- than waiting for answers here,
  obviously...  I have various hardware resource pointers in the
  FAQ, as well.

:  I'll have to check my cables - I had no idea.  I upgraded a
:MicroVAX-II by swapping the CPU and memory cards for a uV-III+ and one
:M7622 card, and now I'd like to add another memory.  I actually don't
:know which cable I've got however it seems to work OK.
:
:  There can't be too much difference in the cables - the header
:connectors on the cards are all the same.


  The wrong cable can and often does lead to run-time hardware errors,
  due to signal integrity issues with the speeds of the MS650-* modules.
  I had no end of problems when I upgraded an old MicroVAX, and it
  tracked back to the cables.

  If you're upgrading, also look carefully at the revisions of the
  other modules -- that was another revision problem I encountered.
  A down-rev QDSS module caused all manner of odd hangs and crashes,
  in my case, as the KA650 and KA655 could push the Q-bus rather
  faster than the down-rev QDSS graphics controller could support.


