Perhaps, but that processor does not exist.

The following is the list of processors for the Alphaserver 4000/4100 family

Module   Variant                                       Clock    On Board Cache
	
B3001-CA                                               300 MHz  None
	
B3002-AB (pass4 EV5 only) replaced by KN304-FA         300 MHz  2 MB
B3004-BA (pass1 or pass2 EV56) replaced by KN304-FA
B3005-BA (pass2 EV56 only) replaced by KN304-FA

B3004-AA (pass1 or pass2 EV56) replaced by KN304-AA    400 MHz  4 MB
B3005-AA (pass2 EV56 only) replaced by KN304-AA

B3004-DA (pass2 EV56 only) replaced by KN304-DA        466 MHz  4 MB
B3005-DA (pass2 EV56 only) replaced by KN304-DA

B3005-CA EV56                                          533 MHz  4 MB
	
B3006-EB EV56                                          600 MHz  8 MB
B3006-EN EV56 (NT only)

> 1) So, with respect to number-crunching: Do you know, or have you
> noticed with your respective systems, if the performance increase
> between a 400MHz cpu and (say) a 600MHz cpu is linear (or better than
> linear) ?

In my experience going from 4MB to 6MB cache is the biggest difference. While I
have not done any exhaustive measuring, I didn't notice a marked increase in
performance in moving from 400MHz to 466MHz, but did notice a nice speedup in
moving to the 600MHz boards with 8MB cache.

> 2) Do I have to change any other hardware if I was to go from my 400MHz
> processors to higher speed ones ?  The HP Retired-Systems-Alpha-4100
> Product Specifications make it seem like you can swap CPU boards and
> reboot.

No other modifications are needed.

Please note that up to 3 power supplies may be used; a single power supply
is sufficient for two CPUs; if you want to use 3 or 4 CPUs, you need
an additional supply (the 3rd supply is for redundancy)

All CPUs must be of the same speed in an multi-CPU configuration for the
Alphaserver 4000/4100 family.

