In article <yto+CN3vAazz@cuebid.zko.hp.com>, brooks@cuebid.zko.hp.nospam (Rob Brooks) writes:
:"Schnootling" <chuckm@dis.wa.gov> writes:
:> I have a dual-cpu 400MHz Alpha 4100. I noticed a 433MHz processor on
:> eBay (for a low price).
:
:Perhaps, but that processor does not exist.

  I suspect our intrepid explorer might be thinking of swapping out
  the Alpha processor itself, and not swapping the processor module
  for another.

:> 1) So, with respect to number-crunching: Do you know, or have you
:> noticed with your respective systems, if the performance increase
:> between a 400MHz cpu and (say) a 600MHz cpu is linear (or better than
:> linear) ?

  Performance does scale, and there are various differences in the 
  implementations of the EV-class processors, too -- you'll want 
  to stay with an EV5 or (better) an EV56 here.  The performance
  boost from 400 to 433 MHz probably isn't worth the cost of the 
  silver heat-sink compound involved, though.  The 400 to 600 MHz
  upgrade is quite noticable.

  When you cross from EV56 to EV6, for instance, the MHz does not
  directly compare; the application performance does not necessarily
  scale from the MHz values across processor families.

  If you are crunching numbers, an EV6 or EV7 wins.  Hands down.

  You can find EV6 systems comparatively inexpensively, and many
  will be rather smaller than the AlphaServer 4100 series box, too.

  The occasional "low-end" HP Itanium system wanders by on eBay, 
  too, and these are as fast as or faster than most Alpha systems.

  I'd not assume I could get an EV56 600 MHz processor to work when
  inserted onto an arbitrary EV56 400 MHz processor module.  You are
  very far off onto your own, should you decide to try this.  If you
  do, check the processor module revisions before you start, and
  make sure your revision has a chance of supporting the new CPU.
  (Assuming it's even the same board, and that's not a safe bet.)


