> I was givne a VAXserver 4000/300.  I think it has VMS on it.  The
> question is, once I get the console wired up to it and power it up,
> how do I recover the login so that I can get into the machine?  There
> has to be a procedure for someone with physical access to the machine
> who has lost their password...

When it powers on, it may boot VMS directly, or it may stop at the "dead
sergeant" prompt. >>>
If it starts booting VMS type control-P.

At the >>> prompt type boot/1 or b/1 (console commands vary somewhat)

you should get to
SYSBOOT>

Type set uafalternate 1
type continue

You should then get a login prompt. Type system as the username, and just
<return> for each password prompt. (there should be two).

(If a highly security minded previous system manager has created a
sys$system:sysuafalt.dat, this process won't work.
There is another more complicated one. It should be on the FAQ.

Once you are in the system, deassign/exec sysuaf, so you are working with
the real sys$system:sysuaf.dat file, then run authorize, and at the UAF>
prompt type

modify system/password=<whatever you want it to be>/nopwdexp
exit

Reboot, and you should now have the system password as you want it.

All this from memory, as I haven't run a real VMS system for several years.

